      "dom_id": "question-101047",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "An arithmetic sequence has the $2$nd term ${a}_{2}=-6$ and the $9$th term ${a}_{9}=-27.$  Calculate the $12$th term.",
      "worked_solution": "The formula for the $n$th term is given by \n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}+(n-1)d,\n$$\n\n \n\nso we need to figure out the first term ${a}_{1}$ and the common difference $d.$ \n\nTo find the common difference, we can find the total difference between the terms ${a}_{2}=-6$ and ${a}_{9}=-27$ and divide it by the number of jumps, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\frac{{a}_{9}-{a}_{2}}{9-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-27-(-6)}{9-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-21}{7} \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use one of our known values, say, ${a}_{2}=-6,$ to determine the first term of the sequence. Since ${a}_{2}$ is $1$ jump after ${a}_{1},$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & ={a}_{1}+d \\\\ -6 & ={a}_{1}+(-3) \\\\ -6 & ={a}_{1}-3 \\\\ {a}_{1} & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the formula for the $n$th term is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =-3+(n-1)\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =-3n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can find the $12$th term by substituting $n=12$ into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{12} & =-3(12) \\\\  & =-36\\end{aligned}\n$$",
